# cflow M6 (step T5): how much of a prompt's card time is the sm_86 F32/BF16 SIMT tile (bf16_tile_gemm + f32_tile_gemm), the
# kernels a tensor-core path (bf16_mma_gemm, today cc 12 only) would speed up? (cflow audit step.md T5, row 7.)
#
# The rule being priced. crates/gpu/src/lib.rs:2249-2271 (`dense_mma_on`: compute-capability major 12 only; the audit's
# lib.rs:2199-2212 has moved) runs the dense prompt sites and the wide BF16 router on the BF16 tile only on cc 12; "an Ampere
# card (compute capability 8.6) keeps the exact F32 products". The tile is 202 registers, one block an SM (audit); the
# tensor path is 126 registers, two blocks. AGENTS.md "Performance first, accuracy opt-in" says the faster path is the default
# and the exact one lives on the verification side; the user's rule for this round: if there is a gain, the sites move.
# Which model's prompt runs them. The sites are (a) the wide router (route_body.rs:830/1340, gpu-deepseek41/src/router.rs:691:
# `wide.enqueue_f32_tile`) and (b) the HC gated block's BF16 matrices (hc_gated.rs:2922, :3035: `HcMatrix::Bf16` ->
# `enqueue_bf16_prompt`) and the dense `Planes::F32/Bf16` sites (site.rs:1579, :1593). On Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (qwen4exp, geo:
# crates/gpu/src/arch/qwen3moe/plan38.rs:35-50: HIDDEN 2560, 4 HC streams so a wide row is 10,240, rank 320, 512 experts, 48
# layers [layer count from the audit: the q38 step has 48 layers]) all three exist; on V4.1 only the router (f32, 384 x 5120)
# and the small HC matrices run them (V4.1's attention projections and experts are K-quants).
# Decomposition [derived], one P = 4096 prompt, A6000:
#   Qwen3.8 flops: router 2 x 4096 x 512 x 2560 x 48 = 0.52 TFLOP; HC matrices, taking 2 sites a layer of rank x wide =
#   320 x 10,240 [assumed: the audit counts 97 HC sites a decode step, ~2 a layer]: 2 x 4096 x 3.28 M x 2 x 48 = 2.58 TFLOP;
#   total ~3.1 TFLOP.
#   tile rate: the A6000's FP32 peak 38.7 TFLOPS x 35..55 % [assumed: no measurement of this kernel's efficiency; one block an SM
#   at 202 registers] = 13.5..21 TFLOPS, so 150..230 ms.
#   prompt window: P 4096 at the 0.3.x Qwen3.8 pp4096 row (docs/numbers.md: 1,662.72 tok/s) = 2.46 s wall; the card's kernel
#   time is some part of that (host experts and NVMe under it), so the share of the card's kernel time is >= the share of the
#   wall: 150..230 / 2460 = 6..9 % of the wall, 7..14 % of the kernel sum [derived]. Band 3..12 % for the unmeasured efficiency.
#   V4.1 (read from M1's trace for free, by kernel name): router 2 x 4096 x 384 x 5120 x 40 = 0.64 TFLOP = 30..45 ms of an 11 s
#   prompt = 0.3..0.4 % of the wall; HC matrices smaller still: under 1 % [derived]. The audit's 2 .. 10 % is Qwen3.8's.
# Resource timeline of the Qwen3.8 prompt [derived, plan.md flow notes]: card SMs busy through the ubatch (grouped GEMMs, the
# tile sites, attention), host union under the card's shadow, PCIe the route's D2H and the sums, NVMe only on the tier layers;
# the wall is the card chain's sum with the host under it, so a cut in a card kernel is a cut in the window up to the host bound.
# What the tensor path would buy [derived]: the tile kernels x (1 - 1/2) on the 3090's GA102 GeForce (half-rate FP32-accum
# tensor) .. x (1 - 1/4) on the A6000, so share s gives +s/2 .. +3s/4 of the card window: 6 % -> +3 .. +4.5 %.
# Runner. nsys-gpu.sh's prefill form on qwen4exp, BLOOMERY_NSYS_N=3 (boundary count K + 2): the window from the first boundary
# launch to the K+1-th, per-kernel table with launches, ms, share of the window's kernel sum; the lcg prompt, so routing
# differs from a real prompt's (the BF16 sites' shapes do not).
# Wall [derived]: Qwen3.8 (111 GB split set, plan (a): host set + card experts) load ~1..3 min (page cache holds the set,
# 234 GB buff/cache seen 2026-10-29) + prompt 4096 ~3 s under nsys + 2 replays + export ~1 min + analysis: 4..7 min.
kind: profile
question: on Qwen3.8's P 4096 prompt on the A6000, what share of the window's kernel time do bf16_tile_gemm and f32_tile_gemm take?
term: share of the prompt window's kernel sum (nsys-gpu.sh prefill form's per-kernel table) in bf16_tile_gemm + f32_tile_gemm,
  Qwen3.8-Flash-Next UD-Q4_K_XL, lcg prompt P 4096, plan as the one visible A6000 resolves it, A6000
unit: %
predict: 3..12
condition: one lease through nsys-gpu.sh with this card, BLOOMERY_MODEL=qwen4exp BLOOMERY_NSYS_FORM=prefill
  BLOOMERY_NSYS_DEPTHS=4096 BLOOMERY_NSYS_N=3, the A6000 the timing card; the table's boundary count equals K + 2 with K read
  from the run's `time prompt ... passes=` row (else no table: void); the `plan`/`load` line names the place and the card's
  expert count (copied into the record); witness pre and post show no compute process on either card and no [cpu-busy]; the
  V4.1 reading by kernel name comes off M1's sqlite with no run of its own
decide-in: 3 percent or more: the tile sites bind a part of the prompt that a tensor path cuts by 2x (3090) to 4x (A6000): the
  gain is share/2 .. 3 share/4 of the card window (+1.5 .. +9 percent in the band); the rule moves the sites to bf16_mma_gemm
  on sm_86, an accuracy-opt-in decision for the lead (activations round to BF16), then a same-lease A/B and the gpu-e2e count
  pin; the audit's row 7 stands
decide-below: under 3 percent (the audit's 2 percent line drops it): the gain is under +1.5 percent of the window at the A6000's
  4x; row 7 is dropped, lib.rs's keep-F32-on-Ampere stays as the default, and the share goes beside the rule as its reason
decide-above: over 12 percent: the tile is slower than the 35 .. 55 percent efficiency assumed, or the window is shorter than
  priced; read the tile's own block-step (ncu-gpu.sh, source form) before pricing the tensor path, because the 2x .. 4x ratio is
  a datasheet ratio and a kernel at low efficiency may be bound by something the tensor path keeps
